ttps://bytebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/two-pointers/pair-sum-=
sorted/image-01-01-2-OIIAKQQK.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div></fi=
gure>
<p>Since 1 is less than the target, we need to <strong>move one of our poin=
ters</strong> to find a new pair with a larger sum.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Left pointer: The left pointer will always point to a value less than or=
 equal to the value at the right pointer because the array is sorted. Incre=
menting it would result in a sum greater than or equal to the current sum o=
f 1.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Right pointer: Decrementing the right pointer would result in a sum that=
=E2=80=99s less than or equal to 1.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Therefore, we should increment the left pointer to find a larger sum:</p=
>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: left;"=
><img alt=3D"Image represents a visual depiction of a two-pointer algorithm=
, likely used to find a pair of numbers in a sorted array that sum to a tar=
