<p>Above, we=E2=80=99ve demonstrated a two-pointer algorithm using inward t=
raversal. Let=E2=80=99s summarize this logic. For any pair of values at <co=
de>left</code> and <code>right</code>:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>If their sum is less than the target, increment <code>left</code>, aimin=
g to increase the sum towards the target value.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If their sum is greater than the target, decrement <code>right</code>, a=
iming to decrease the sum towards the target value.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If their sum is equal to the target value, return <code>[left, right]</c=
ode>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>We can stop moving the <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> pointers=
 when they meet, as this indicates no pair summing to the target was found.=
</p>
